course: myths
lesson: 36
title: Olympiad: which claim is true? ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: This olympiad block mixes the whole course into puzzles: four friends, four claims, and usually only one of them survives a careful look.
explain: The trick is never to trust the loudest voice; check each claim against what you already know, then count, turn or line things up to be sure.
explain: Picture the quiz card: "Amy says camels keep water in their humps, Ben says bats are blind, Cara says owls can turn their heads three-quarters of a circle." Only Cara is right, and you can prove the other two wrong.
explain: Watch the wording too: "a million times" is hyperbole and is never a number to count, while "a bit weaker" can be a plain, true understatement.
explain: Some puzzles have two right halves: Everest and Mauna Kea can both be "the tallest" until you ask, measured from where?
fact: A camel's hump stores fat, not water; all bats can see; an owl can turn its head about 270 degrees, three-quarters of a circle.
fact: Sound travels about one kilometre every three seconds, so counting the seconds between a flash and its thunder tells you how far away the lightning is.
fact: Spiders have eight legs and insects such as ants have six; a ladybird's spots show its kind, so a seven-spot ladybird always has seven.
fact: The Earth is nearest the Sun in early January, when it is winter in Britain and summer in Australia, so distance cannot be what makes summer.
fact: At full moon the whole near side of the Moon is lit and the far side is dark; at new moon the far side is fully lit, so the far side is not always dark.
fact: The last big dinosaurs died out about 66 million years ago and humans appeared only about 300,000 years ago, so no person ever met a living big dinosaur.
fact: In the Salem witch trials of 1692 nineteen people were hanged and one man was crushed with stones; nobody was burned there.
fact: Vikings sailed to North America around the year 1000; horned Viking helmets were first made for an opera in 1876, almost 900 years later.
fact: Gravity at the International Space Station is about 90% as strong as on the ground; astronauts float because they are falling round the Earth.
fact: Mount Everest has the highest peak above sea level, 8,849 metres; from its base on the sea floor, Mauna Kea in Hawaii is over 10,000 metres tall.
fact: On ordinary flat world maps Greenland looks about as big as Africa, but Africa is about 14 times bigger.
fact: Only honeybees usually die after stinging people; bumblebees and wasps can sting more than once.
fact: Books printed before 2006 list nine planets because Pluto was called a planet until then; that is out of date, not a lie.
exercise: Amy: "Camels store water in their humps." Ben: "Bats are blind." Cara: "Owls turn their heads about three-quarters of a circle." Who is right? | Only Cara | Only Amy | Only Ben | All three of them
exercise: An owl faces forward, then turns its head 270 degrees to its right. Which way is it now looking? | To its left | Straight behind it | To its right | Straight ahead again
exercise: You see a flash and count 12 seconds before the thunder. About how far away is the lightning? | About 4 kilometres | About 12 kilometres | About 36 kilometres | About 3 kilometres
exercise: A jar holds 3 spiders and 2 ants. How many legs are in the jar altogether? | 36 | 30 | 24 | 42
exercise: Tom says his seven-spot ladybird is three weeks old, so next week it will have eight spots. How many will it have? | Seven, because spots show its kind, not its age | Eight, one new spot for every week of age | Ten, as ladybirds grow faster in summer | Fewer, because old spots slowly fade away
exercise: Which of these four is the only TRUE claim? | Lightning often strikes the same tall building | Bats are completely blind | Goldfish forget everything after three seconds | Shooting stars are real stars falling to the Earth
exercise: Which one is the odd one out, and why? | A wasp, because it can sting you twice | A honeybee, because it is an insect | A bumblebee, because it is furry | A honeybee, because it can make honey
exercise: In early January the Earth is nearest the Sun. Which fact shows distance does not cause summer? | It is winter in Britain then | It is summer in Australia then | The Sun rises later in January | January has thirty-one days in it
exercise: At full moon, which part of the Moon is in the dark? | The far side | The near side | Both sides | Neither side
exercise: At new moon, how much of the "dark side" of the Moon is lit by the Sun? | All of it | None of it | Only the half nearest us | Just a thin crescent
exercise: How many people were burned in the Salem witch trials of 1692? | None | Nineteen | Twenty | One
exercise: About how long after the Viking voyages to America were horned helmets made for an opera? | Almost 900 years | About 90 years | About 9 years | About 9,000 years
exercise: A cartoon shows a caveman riding a big dinosaur. About how long was it between the last big dinosaurs and the first humans? | More than 65 million years | About 300,000 years | About 66 years | No time, they lived together
exercise: Maya says "Everest is the tallest mountain"; Jake says "Mauna Kea is". Who is right? | Both, if they measure from different places | Only Maya, because Everest is in all the books | Only Jake, because Hawaii is nearer the Sun | Neither, because the tallest is in Antarctica
exercise: On a classroom map Greenland and Africa look about the same size. What is true? | Africa is about 14 times bigger | They really are the same size | Greenland is a bit bigger than Africa | Africa is about twice as big
exercise: Two honest books: one from 1998 lists nine planets, one from 2015 lists eight. Why? | Pluto became a dwarf planet in 2006 | A planet blew up between 1998 and 2015 | The 1998 book was written by people who lied | The 2015 book left one out by mistake
test: "nan's told me a MILLION times cheese gives u nightmares" What is really going on in this line? | A hyperbole for "often", and a claim with no good evidence | Nan has said it exactly one million times, so it's true | It is a proven fact that she repeats a lot | Nan is just joking, and she has never said it at all
test: Astronaut on the radio: "Gravity up here is a bit weaker than at home." Kid: "there's NO gravity in space innit." Who is right? | The astronaut: it's about 90% as strong | The kid: there is no gravity at all in space | Both: it changes every single hour | Neither: it is twice as strong up there
test: Jess, from Newcastle: "Howay, a wasp stung us twice, it should be deed after the first one, man." What's true? | Wasps can sting more than once | It must have been two wasps then | Every insect dies after its first sting | She was stung by a honeybee twice
test: Four claims on a quiz sheet; three are myths. Which one is true? | Lightning hits the Empire State Building about 20 to 25 times a year | The Great Wall is easy to see from orbit with bare eyes | People in Columbus's time all thought the Earth was flat | Glass is a slow liquid that runs down old windows
test: "lowkey sus but my cousin swears the dark side of the Moon is ALWAYS dark, no cap" What is the best reply? | It is lit at new moon; "dark" once meant unknown | He's right, the Sun never reaches that side | It is dark only in winter, and light in summer | It glows by itself, so it is never dark at all
test: Put these in order, oldest first: Columbus lands, Stonehenge begun, Wright brothers fly, Pluto made a dwarf planet. | Stonehenge, Columbus, Wright brothers, Pluto | Columbus, Stonehenge, Wright brothers, Pluto | Stonehenge, Wright brothers, Columbus, Pluto | Stonehenge, Columbus, Pluto, Wright brothers
test: Grandad: "That flat map's not ideal, Greenland's a tad oversized." Is he exaggerating, playing it down, or exact? | Playing it down: Africa is about 14 times bigger | Exaggerating: Greenland is really bigger than Africa | Exact: the map is only slightly off | Exaggerating: maps are always perfect
test: "Australia gets summer in January cos we're closest to the Sun then, sorted" Which reply cracks the argument fastest? | Then why is Britain having winter on the same day? | Then why is the sea salty all year round? | Then why does Australia have such big hot deserts? | Then why do we have twelve months in a year?
test: Lily's book says nine planets, Sam's says eight; Sam yells "your book's lying!" What is fairest? | Her book is just out of date: Pluto changed in 2006 | Her book is lying and should be thrown away | Both books are wrong: there are ten planets | Sam's book is the old one, so he's wrong
test: Kid at the zoo: "Ostriches bury their heads, bats are blind, and owls can look straight behind them!" How many of the three are true? | One: owls can look behind them | None of them at all | Two of them | All three of them
test: In a jar there are 2 spiders, 1 ant and 1 seven-spot ladybird. Your mate says "that's 28 legs and 7 spots, easy". Is he right? | Yes: 16 plus 6 plus 6 legs, and 7 spots | No: spiders have 6 legs, so it's 24 legs | No: ladybirds are spiders, so it's 30 legs | No: the spots depend on age, so it's 9
exam: Determine which of the following assertions is consistent with established facts. | An owl is able to rotate its head through approximately three-quarters of a circle | A camel's hump serves as a reservoir of water for the desert | All species of bat are without sight | A goldfish retains memories for three seconds only
exam: It is given that thunder is heard nine seconds after a flash. Calculate the approximate distance of the lightning. | Three kilometres | Nine kilometres | Twenty-seven kilometres | One kilometre
exam: Identify the observation which demonstrates that the distance of the Earth from the Sun does not determine the seasons. | Britain experiences winter when the Earth is nearest the Sun | Australia experiences summer in January, when the Earth is nearest | The Sun appears yellow from the ground | The Moon is visible during the daytime
exam: Ascertain the number of persons burned in the Salem witch trials of 1692. | None | Nineteen | Twenty | Two
